The unfiltered ratio fails for the non-integrable chains. The current spectral moments support \(c_2(N)\sim K/N\to0\), although \(N\le22\) is still far from that asymptotic regime.

There is also a correction to the integrable premise. Conservation of \(J_{\rm tot}\) removes the leading \(1/N\) contamination, but does not guarantee \(c_2\to c\). For the site-centered critical Ising density, I find analytically
\[
c_2(N)\longrightarrow \frac9{98},
\]
despite \(J_{\rm tot}|I\rangle=0\).

The code is saved as [ks_test.py](ks_test.py). The [full report](ks_report.md) contains the tables, fits, and supplementary checks; the numerical data are in [results_extended.json](results_extended.json). Everything was calculated closed book in the working directory, within the runtime budget.

For the analytic estimate, write \(q=4\pi/N\) and \(h(q)=h_{-2}\). Continuity gives
\[
i[H,h(q)]=-(1-e^{-iq})J(q),
\]
and hence, for an excited state of energy \(\omega_a=E_a-E_0\),
\[
\omega_a\langle a|h(q)|I\rangle
=i(1-e^{-iq})\langle a|J(q)|I\rangle.
\]
For the densities used here,
\[
J_{\rm tot}=i\sum_j\sum_{r>0}r[h_j,h_{j+r}],
\]
with only \(r=1,2\) contributing.

Define the high-energy inverse moments
\[
A_{-p}=
\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac1N
\sum_{a\in{\rm UV}}
\frac{|\langle a|J_{\rm tot}|I\rangle|^2}{\omega_a^p}.
\]
The UV sum can be defined with a fixed positive energy cutoff. \(A_0\) is the high-energy part of \(\operatorname{Var}(J_{\rm tot})/N\).

Assuming the high-energy spectral measure is smooth at \(q=0\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
m_0^{\rm UV}
&=4\sin^2(q/2)\,N[A_{-2}+o(1)]
=\frac{16\pi^2A_{-2}}N+o(N^{-1}),\\
m_1^{\rm UV}
&=4\sin^2(q/2)\,N[A_{-1}+o(1)]
=\frac{16\pi^2A_{-1}}N+o(N^{-1}).
\end{aligned}
\]
These dominate the CFT contributions of orders \(N^{-2}\) and \(N^{-3}\). Consequently,
\[
\boxed{\displaystyle
c_2(N)\sim\frac{K}{N},\qquad
K=128\pi^2\frac{A_{-2}^{\,3}}{A_{-1}^{\,2}}.}
\]
The current variance alone does not determine \(K\); its inverse-energy moments are required.

If \(J_{\rm tot}\) commutes with \(H\), uniqueness of the ground state implies zero current variance. This removes the terms above. It can still leave \(m_0^{\rm UV}=O(N^{-3})\) and \(m_1^{\rm UV}=O(N^{-3})\). The latter competes with the CFT contribution.

The low-energy normalization follows directly. Setting \(\alpha=2\pi v/N\),
\[
w_T=|\langle T|h_{-2}|I\rangle|^2=\frac c2\alpha^2,
\qquad \Delta_T=2\alpha,
\]
so
\[
\boxed{\displaystyle c_{\rm MV}=8w_T/\Delta_T^2.}
\]
There is no additional \(2\pi/N\) factor. The target momentum is relative to the ground momentum, \(P_T=P_0+4\pi/N\).

For the numerical tables, I used the site-centered Ising/OF density
\[
h_j=-\frac12(X_{j-1}X_j+X_jX_{j+1})-Z_j
+\lambda(X_{j-1}X_jZ_{j+1}+Z_{j-1}X_jX_{j+1}).
\]
For the spin chains I used the displayed forward-bond summands. The literal Ising density is reported separately below.

All ground states were unique for \(N=8,10,\ldots,22\). I searched both parities and every momentum for Ising/OF, and every momentum in \(S^z=0\) for the SU(2) chains. Every integer-spin multiplet has an \(S^z=0\) representative. The spin-chain ground momentum is \(\pi\) for \(N=2\bmod4\), and zero otherwise. The Ising ground momentum is zero.

Translation eigenvector residuals were below \(4.4\times10^{-15}\), and ground-state eigenvector residuals below \(1.6\times10^{-11}\). Zeros in the variance column denote numerical roundoff, below \(1.3\times10^{-28}\).

