To estimate the asymptotic coefficients, I computed the projected current resolvents
\[
A_{-1}(N)=\frac{\langle j|(H-E_0)^{-1}|j\rangle}{N},
\qquad
A_{-2}(N)=\frac{\|(H-E_0)^{-1}|j\rangle\|^2}{N},
\]
where \(|j\rangle=(J_{\rm tot}-\langle J_{\rm tot}\rangle)|I\rangle\). These include all excited states; identifying their limits with the UV moments assumes the low-energy current contribution vanishes.

Fits of these moments to \(C+b/N^2+d/N^4\), using \(N=14,\ldots,22\), give

| Model | Limiting variance/site | \(a\) in \(m_0\sim a/N\) | \(d\) in \(m_1\sim d/N\) | \(K=8a^3/d^2\) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| OF \(0.2\) | 0.01403394 | 0.03803650 | 0.28566191 | 0.00539495 |
| OF \(0.35\) | 0.03776324 | 0.11627186 | 0.80095630 | 0.01960181 |
| \(J_1\)-\(J_2\) | 0.00743247 | 0.21006892 | 0.47547904 | 0.32802929 |

Changing the lower fit cutoff from 12 to 16 gives \(K\) ranges \(0.005391\)–\(0.005403\), \(0.019546\)–\(0.019643\), and \(0.327868\)–\(0.328111\). These measure fit-window sensitivity, not rigorous uncertainties.

The displayed \(c_2\) values are not yet proportional to \(1/N\). For example, OF \(0.2\) has \(c_2(22)=0.2599\), whereas its inferred asymptote is \(0.005395/22\). Subleading contributions dominate these sizes. A direct \(K/N\) fit would therefore be misleading.

For \(c_{\rm MV}\), fits to \(C+b/N^2+d/N^4\) on \(N=14,\ldots,22\) give

| Model | \(C\) | \(b\) | \(d\) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Ising, centered | 0.499997 | −7.3991 | 38.6445 |
| Heisenberg | 1.002282 | 23.0368 | 338.4935 |
| OF \(0.2\) | 0.497957 | 9.1973 | −37.0018 |
| OF \(0.35\), largest overlap | 0.407370 | 79.8833 | −6295.2636 |
| \(J_1\)-\(J_2\) | 1.000473 | 33.2736 | 681.6547 |

The OF \(0.35\) extrapolation is unreliable because the largest-overlap rule selects the second state, rather than the lowest stress-tensor candidate. Supplementary calculations give

| \(N\) | Largest-overlap estimate | Lowest-state estimate |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 20 | 0.569018 | 0.588676 |
| 22 | 0.544475 | 0.576694 |
| 24 | 0.520935 | 0.566675 |

Fitting the lowest-state estimate over \(14,\ldots,24\) gives \(C=0.510962\). Changing the lower cutoff from 12 to 16 changes this to \(0.516254\)–\(0.506819\). This supports convergence to \(1/2\), but the requested largest-overlap prescription has not reached reliable asymptotic state selection.

The Ising convention deserves separate treatment. For the literal density
\[
h_j=-X_jX_{j+1}-Z_j,
\]
the continuity current is
\[
J_{\rm tot}=2\sum_jX_jY_{j+1},
\]
which is not conserved. Its variance per site is 4.

| \(N\) | \(c_2\) | \(m_0N^2\) | \(m_1N^3\) | \(\operatorname{Var}(J)/N\) | \(c_{\rm MV}\) |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 8 | 1.162933 | 188.935007 | 6811.399064 | 4 | 0.768588 |
| 10 | 1.152617 | 268.334394 | 11580.222767 | 4 | 0.813299 |
| 12 | 1.107015 | 347.930918 | 17446.481208 | 4 | 0.836924 |
| 14 | 1.050170 | 427.515072 | 24397.329968 | 4 | 0.850945 |
| 16 | 0.991717 | 507.035898 | 32427.172290 | 4 | 0.859954 |
| 18 | 0.935562 | 586.486848 | 41533.267160 | 4 | 0.866089 |
| 20 | 0.883176 | 665.873902 | 51714.147699 | 4 | 0.870457 |
| 22 | 0.834960 | 745.205805 | 62968.972574 | 4 | 0.873678 |

Here the free-fermion calculation gives
\[
A_{-2}=\frac14,\qquad A_{-1}\to\frac8{3\pi},
\]
and therefore
\[
m_0\sim\frac{4\pi^2}{N},\qquad
m_1\sim\frac{128\pi}{3N},\qquad
c_2\sim\frac{9\pi^4}{32N}.
\]
The largest-overlap low-energy state is also not the stress tensor for this density; that prescription tends to \(8/9\).

For the centered density, instead,
\[
J_{\rm tot}=\sum_j(X_jY_{j+1}-Y_jX_{j+1})
\]
is conserved. Nevertheless, its high-energy pair form factor obeys
\[
|F(k,q)|^2
=4[1-\cos(q/2)]^2\cos^2(k/2-q/4)
=\frac{q^4}{16}\cos^2(k/2)+O(q^5).
\]
Using \(\epsilon(k)=4\sin(k/2)\) for \(0<k<2\pi\), and the factor \(1/2\) that removes duplicate Majorana pairs,
\[
\begin{aligned}
m_0^{\rm UV}
&\sim\frac N2\frac{q^4}{16}
\int_0^{2\pi}\frac{dk}{2\pi}\cos^2(k/2)
=\frac{Nq^4}{64},\\
m_1^{\rm UV}
&\sim\frac N2\frac{q^4}{16}
\int_0^{2\pi}\frac{dk}{2\pi}
8\sin(k/2)\cos^2(k/2)
=\frac{Nq^4}{6\pi}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Adding the CFT contributions with \(v=2\), \(c=1/2\), gives
\[
m_0=\frac{4\pi^2}{N^2}+\frac{4\pi^4}{N^3}+O(N^{-4}),
\qquad
m_1=\frac{224\pi^3}{3N^3}+o(N^{-3}).
\]
Thus
\[
\boxed{\displaystyle
c_2=\frac9{98}+\frac{27\pi^2}{98N}+O(N^{-2}).}
\]
The independent free-fermion sum gives \(c_2(100000)=0.0918639284\), approaching \(9/98=0.0918367347\). In contrast,
\[
c_{\rm MV}
=\frac12\left[\frac{\cos(\pi/N)}{\cos(\pi/(2N))}\right]^4
\longrightarrow\frac12.
\]

For Heisenberg, the available \(c_2\) values are close to one, but do not establish that limit. A supplementary \(n=1\) calculation, whose CFT vacuum contribution vanishes, detects residual spectral weight. Current conservation alone cannot settle the limiting correction, particularly with marginal logarithms.

The supported conclusion is that the non-integrable unfiltered ratios tend to zero under the stated spectral-limit assumptions. Low-energy projection gives estimates consistent with the expected central charges, with a substantial state-selection crossover at OF \(0.35\). Integrability, or conservation of the total energy current, is insufficient by itself to validate the unfiltered formula.

